:: stage3-tutorial2-f-vn-transcript-eng-ks1 [nobr]

<p>I love postcards about our local area because it gets us thinking about what we have around us - appreciating what makes it special and highlighting where it could improve to make everyone in that community happier. </p>

<p>All of our communities are special in their own way - and they've got to being that way thanks to the people who are part of those communities. People like you who come up with bold, brilliant and slightly silly ideas to make the community happier. </p>


<p>
    Lionel is always on the reception desk of our library, he is always thinking of ways to make our community feel welcome. It's a never ending vision we all commit to in the Library - a space for all. We all can play our part in making a community feel welcoming to others. 
    Even small acts can make a big difference. <br/>
    Lionel knows how to say hello in 26 languages and I’m trying to learn too!
</p>

<p>
    It’s small but it means the world to our community. <br/>
    I mean, I don’t even know how you say hello in your area. 
</p>

<p>
    In England some say Hello. <br/>
    In Welsh - It's Shwmae!<br/>
    You may hear someone in Scotland say " Madainn Mhath" which is good morning in Scottish Gaelic.<br/>
    In Northern Ireland, some people speak Irish or Ulster Scots and say Dia Duit!<br/>
</p>

<p>Then I know, Czesc, Assalam-o-alaikum, Ola, Buna, Ciao, Sat Sri Akal. Languages are wonderful. </p>

<p> Maybe you have lots of different ways of saying hello - try them out!  
</p>
<p>Right, you’ve probably got lots to get on with, so come back when you want another Dream Team video or if you are ready to send your Dreams to the BFG. </p>

<p>In the meantime, why not try to learn a new way to say hello whilst I say goodbye. </p>

<p>Well done again!</p>

:: stage3-tutorial2-f-vn-transcript-eng-ks2 [nobr]

<p>I love the idea of Day Dreamers Visitor’s Guides - not just because I came up with it - but also because it gets us thinking about what we have around us - appreciating what makes it special and highlighting where it could improve to make everyone in that community happier. </p>

<p>All of our communities are special in their own way - and they've got to being that way thanks to the people who are part of those communities. People like you who come up with bold, brilliant and slightly outrageous ideas to make the community happier. </p>

<p>
    Lionel is always on the reception desk of our library, he is always thinking of ways to make our community feel welcome. It's a never ending vision we all commit to in the Library - a space for all. We all can play our part in making a community feel welcoming to others. 
    Even small acts can make a big difference. <br/>
    Lionel knows how to say hello in 26 languages and I’m trying to learn too!
</p>

<p>
    It’s small but it means the world to our community. <br/>
    I mean, I don’t even know how you say hello in your area. 
</p>

<p>
    In England some say Hello. <br/>
    In Welsh - It's Shwmae!<br/>
    You may hear someone in Scotland say " Madainn Mhath" which is good morning in Scottish Gaelic.<br/>
    In Northern Ireland, some people speak Irish or Ulster Scots and say Dia Duit!<br/>
</p>

<p>Then I know, Czesc, Assalam-o-alaikum, Ola, Buna, Ciao, Sat Sri Akal. Languages are wonderful. </p>

<p> Maybe you have lots of different ways of saying hello - try them out!  
</p>
<p>Right, you’ve probably got lots to get on with, so come back when you want another Dream Team video or if you are ready to send your Dreams to the BFG. </p>

<p>In the meantime, why not try to learn a new way to say hello whilst I say goodbye. </p>

<p>Well done again!</p>

